{0 Type-safe router}

{1 Declaring routes}

Routes live in one manifest file compiled by [server-reason-react.router-gen].
The manifest is a single [Router.make] expression:

{[
Router.make(
  ~basePath="/demo/router",
  ~search={ text: Router.Search.optional(string) },
  ~layout=Pages.AppLayout,
  ~loading=Pages.GlobalLoading,
  ~error=Pages.AppError,
  ~errorStatus=Pages.AppError.status,
  ~notFound=Pages.NotFound,
  [
    Router.route(Home, ~page=Pages.App, ~path="/"),
    Router.route(NewNote, ~page=Pages.NewNote, ~path="/new", ~loading=Pages.NewNoteLoading),
    Router.group(
      ~path="/:id<NoteId.t>",
      ~layout=Pages.NoteLayout,
      ~loader=Pages.NoteLoader,
      ~loaderLabel=note,
      [
        Router.route(Note, ~page=Pages.Note, ~path="/", ~loading=Pages.NoteLoading),
        Router.route(EditNote, ~page=Pages.EditNote, ~path="/edit", ~loading=Pages.EditNoteLoading),
      ],
    ),
  ],
);
]}

[Router.route] declares a leaf page with a variant name. [Router.group] nests
routes under a shared path prefix and layout. Both accept attachments, as does
the root.

[Router.redirect] declares an inbound redirect, temporary by default, without adding a
route constructor or link helper to the generated application API:

{[
Router.redirect(
  ~path="/old-notes/:id<NoteId.t>",
  ~target=(~id, ()) => Router.Note.destination(~id, ()),
)
]}

Add [~permanent=true] for a permanent redirect. Document requests then
receive status 308, so the request method survives the redirect. A temporary
redirect answers documents with a see-other response. A client navigation
receives the redirect envelope in both cases.

The destination callback receives the redirect's decoded path parameters,
including those inherited from parent groups, and its search record as
[~search]. Redirects inside groups inherit their path schema, but do not run
group loaders or attachments. The search record has the root parameters and
the redirect's own. Without its own parameters it has the type
[Router.search]. With its own parameters, its type is local to the registry,
so build a route's record from its fields:

{[
Router.redirect(
  ~path="/old-search",
  ~search={ q: Router.Search.optional(string) },
  ~target=(~search, ()) =>
    Router.Home.destination(~search=Router.Home.search(~text=?search.q, ()), ()),
)
]}

Use [Router.unsafeDestination] when the target is outside the generated route
manifest or must preserve a legacy URL exactly:

{[
Router.redirect(
  ~path="/docs",
  ~target=() => Router.unsafeDestination("https://docs.example.com"),
)
]}

[unsafeDestination] does not validate or encode its argument. Prefer a
generated route destination when the router owns the target URL.

Attachments take modules, and the generator resolves the convention inside
each module:

- [~page], [~layout], [~loading], [~notFound], [~invalidSearch] resolve
  [Module.make], a component.
- [~loader] resolves [Module.load]. The scope's components receive the loader
  result under the module name ([Pages.NoteLoader] becomes [~noteLoader]), or
  under the [~loaderLabel] name when given.
- [~error] resolves a boundary component, at the root as on groups and routes.
- [~errorStatus], on [Router.make] only, is a function from an application
  error to a [Router.Status.t]. Without it, an application error answers 500.
- [~metadata] and [~headers] are plain functions, not modules.

{2 Prefetch levels}

[~prefetch] on [Router.route] or [Router.group] sets how eagerly the client
may prefetch the route. The levels, from least to most eager, are [Off],
[Intent], [Viewport] and [Idle]. A route without [~prefetch] takes the level
of its nearest group that sets one, and [Intent] when none does. A route's own
[~prefetch] overrides its groups. [Router.make] and [Router.redirect] do not
take [~prefetch].

{[
Router.group(
  ~path="/settings",
  ~prefetch=Viewport,
  [
    Router.route(Settings, ~page=Pages.Settings, ~path="/"),
    Router.route(Billing, ~page=Pages.Billing, ~path="/billing", ~prefetch=Off),
  ],
)
]}

Every generated destination carries its route's level, so the client reads it
without a request: [RouterRuntime.prefetchLevel(Router.Settings.destination())]
is [Viewport]. Other destinations are [Intent];
[RouterRuntime.withPrefetch] sets a level on one. [RouterRuntime.PrefetchLevel]
has [toString] and [fromString] for the lower-case names [off], [intent],
[viewport] and [idle], and [compare], which orders the levels by eagerness.

A navigation request with the header [Router-Prefetch: <level>] is a
prefetch. When that level is more eager than the route's level, the server
answers 204 with [Router-Prefetch-Refused: <route level>] and runs no loader.
Otherwise it answers exactly as it answers the navigation. Every navigation
reply carries [Vary: Accept, Router-Prefetch], so a shared cache never
answers a prefetch with a stored navigation or the other way round. A path
that matches no route answers as a navigation does. An adapter passes the header's level to [Server.run] as
[?prefetch]. A refusal is the outcome [PrefetchRefused(level)], and
[Adapter.reply] turns it into the 204. [DreamRouter] does this for RSC
requests and ignores a header value that is not a level.

{2 Names and labels}

A route name is a bare constructor, such as [Note]. A dotted name is an error.
Each route name must be unique, and these names are reserved because the
generated module already uses them: [Status], [Loader], [Error], [Metadata],
[Headers], [Navigation], [Search], and [Link].

Path parameter names and loader result labels become labelled arguments of
the generated helpers. Within one branch they must be unique. These labels
belong to the generated API and cannot be used: [applicationStatus],
[ariaCurrent], [basePath], [children], [className], [decodeError],
[destination], [download], [error], [fallback], [hash], [history], [href],
[input], [item], [make], [pattern], [registry], [resetScroll], [revalidate],
[routeSearch], [search], [target], [updateSearch], [unsafeDestination],
[useNavigation], [useRoute], [useSearch], [useUpdateHash], [value], and
[values]. The
generator reports a conflict at the route that uses the label.

Search parameters are fields of one [search] record, not labelled arguments,
so these names do not apply to them: a search parameter can be called
[target], [error], [input] or [hash]. Search parameter names must be unique
within a branch, and a path parameter may share a name with one.

{2 Path syntax}

[~path] is an absolute path made of slash-separated segments. Use ["/"] for an
empty path. A group path is a prefix for its child group and route paths, so
["/notes"] and ["/:id<int>"] compose to ["/notes/:id<int>"]. Each path must
start with [/]. Empty segments, trailing slashes, [.] segments, and [..]
segments are invalid.

A segment is one of:

- A static value such as [notes], [new], or [café]. Write decoded text in the
  manifest. The router percent-encodes it when it builds an href. A static
  segment can contain letters, digits, valid UTF-8, and URI path punctuation,
  but it cannot contain [/], [%], [?], or [#].
- A typed parameter in the form [:name<type>]. The parameter must occupy the
  complete segment. The name follows Reason labelled-argument syntax. [bool],
  [int], and [string] are built in. A custom type must have the form
  [Module.t].
- A final catch-all in the form [:name<string...>]. It matches one or more
  remaining segments and the page receives them as [list(string)]. It must be
  the last segment of the route path, it cannot appear in a group path, and it
  never matches zero segments: [/assets/:parts<string...>] does not match
  [/assets].
- An optional parameter in the form [:name?<type>]. The route matches with
  and without the segment. The page receives [option(type)], and the
  generated helpers take an optional labelled argument [?name]. When the
  argument is [None], the href leaves out the segment.
- A final optional catch-all in the form [:name?<string...>]. It matches zero
  or more remaining segments. The page receives [list(string)], empty when no
  segment remains. The generated helpers take [?name: list(string)], and an
  empty list renders no segment: [/docs/:rest?<string...>] gives [/docs] and
  [/docs/a/b].

The [?] after the name marks the segment as optional, like the [?name]
argument it produces. Optional segments are allowed only in a [Router.route]
path. A group path and a [Router.redirect] path cannot contain them. Mixed
segments such as [file-:id<int>] are not supported. Put search values after
[?] in a destination through [~search], rather than in [~path].

A route with optional segments matches one concrete pattern for each
combination of present and absent optional segments:
[/help/:lang?<string>/topics/:id<int>] matches [/help/topics/:id<int>] and
[/help/:lang<string>/topics/:id<int>]. Each concrete pattern follows the
precedence and conflict rules below. The generator reports an error when two
concrete patterns of one route can match the same pathname, as in
[/docs/:a?<string>/:b?<string>], and when a concrete pattern duplicates or
overlaps another route: [/:a?<string>/x] next to [/x] is a duplicate, and
[/docs/:rest?<string...>] next to a [/docs] route is a duplicate.

[~basePath] uses the static path syntax and cannot contain parameters. The
registry rejects duplicate paths and patterns that can match the same pathname
with equal specificity. This includes crossing catch-all patterns such as
[/foo/:left<string...>] and [/:scope<string>/bar/:right<string...>]. When an
overlap has a unique winner, the pattern with more static segments takes
precedence, and a route without a catch-all takes precedence over a catch-all
route with the same static count. When one route path is a prefix of another,
parameters at the same position must use the same name and type.

For example, with [/docs/:rest?<string...>], [/docs/:topic<string>], and
[/docs/api/:name<string>]: [/docs] matches the catch-all with an empty list,
[/docs/intro] matches [/docs/:topic<string>], [/docs/api/list] matches
[/docs/api/:name<string>], and [/docs/a/b] matches the catch-all.

{3 Trailing slashes}

Generated hrefs never end with a slash, so the canonical form of every route
is slash-free. [~trailingSlash] on [Router.make] sets the policy for request
pathnames that end with one or more slashes:

- [Redirect], the default, answers with a permanent redirect to the canonical
  pathname. Document requests receive status 308, so the request method
  survives the redirect. The query string is preserved.
- [Reject] treats the request as not found.

Under [Redirect], a pathname that still starts with [//] after the trailing
slashes are removed, such as [//host/], is not found. A redirect to it would
be a protocol-relative URL that points at another host.

{3 Path parameter codecs}

The router decodes a segment written [:name<Module.t>] with [Module.parse]
and prints it with [Module.to_string]:

{[
/* NoteId.re */
type t = int;
let parse = value =>
  switch (int_of_string_opt(value)) {
  | Some(id) when id >= 0 => Ok(id)
  | _ => Error("expected a note id")
  };
let to_string = string_of_int;
]}

Codecs receive percent-decoded segments. The router owns URL encoding. [bool],
[int], and [string] are built in. [bool] accepts exactly [true] and [false].
[int] accepts an optional [-] followed by ASCII digits, within the 32-bit
signed range. It rejects [+5], [0x10], [1_000], and larger values on native and
in the browser alike. Search parameters use the same [int] codec.
[to_string] must return one non-empty, valid UTF-8 path segment. Destination
construction raises [Invalid_argument] for an empty value, [/], [.], [..],
control characters, or invalid UTF-8. Valid output is percent-encoded. A failed
parse produces [InvalidPathParameter].

{2 Search parameters}

[Router.make], [Router.route] and [Router.redirect] declare search parameters
as a typed record. [Router.group] cannot declare them: put shared parameters
on [Router.make], or repeat them on each route.

{[
~search={
  page: Router.Search.default(int, 1),
  filter: Router.Search.optional(Filter.t),
  tags: Router.Search.many(string),
  token: Router.Search.required(string),
}
]}

A route or redirect sees the root parameters as well as its own. Canonical
URLs omit values that equal their declared default. A required root search
parameter together with a root [~error] boundary also requires [~invalidSearch].

The field name is the query key, so a key must be a valid record label.

{3 The search record}

The generator turns the search parameters into records that hold decoded
values: [required(t)] is [t], [optional(t)] is [option(t)], [default(t, d)]
is [t] and [many(t)] is [list(t)]. [Router.search] holds the root parameters.
Each route with search parameters has [Router.Route.search], with the root
parameters followed by the route's own. A route without its own parameters
re-exports [Router.search], so a [Router.useSearch] record passes to it as
is. A route that adds parameters has its own type: build it with the
generated constructor, which takes each parameter as a labelled argument,
with optional ones as [?name]:

{[
Router.SiteInspector.search(~target="example.com", ())
]}

Generated functions and route components take the record as [?search], or
as [~search] when a parameter is required. An absent [?search] means every
parameter at its default. A route without search parameters takes no
[search] argument:

{[
<Router.SiteInspector target="_blank" search={Router.SiteInspector.search(~target="example.com", ())}>
  children
</Router.SiteInspector>

Router.Home.href(~search={text: Some("notes")}, ())
]}

A record literal lists every field, root fields included. Inside a JSX prop,
it needs the generated interface, because the component's props function
does not carry the field types. Generate [Router.mli] with
[--mode interface] next to [Router.ml], as in the Libraries section. Without
it, qualify one field ([{Router.Home.text: None}]) or use the constructor.

Each search type also has [search_to_rsc] and [search_of_rsc], so a client
component can take a search record as a prop:

{[
[@react.client.component]
let make = (~search: Router.SiteInspector.search) =>
  <span> {React.string(search.target)} </span>;
]}

Attachments and pages receive the record of their scope as [~search] (see
Loaders).

{3 Reading a query}

Server and client read a query string with the same rules, so one URL has
the same search values on both sides:

- The leading [?] is optional. Pairs are split on [&], and empty pairs are
  skipped.
- A pair splits at its first [=]. A pair without [=] has the value [""].
- [+] is a space and [%2B] is a plus. Hex digits may be upper or lower case.
- A [%] not followed by two hex digits, or escapes that do not decode to valid
  UTF-8 (such as [%FF], overlong forms or encoded surrogates), make the whole
  query invalid.
- A name keeps all its values in order. Names keep the order of their first
  appearance.
- A query longer than 8192 bytes is invalid.

The server answers an invalid query with status 400 and the [~invalidSearch]
boundary, or the root error page when there is none. When Back or Forward reaches an entry whose query is invalid,
for example after a [history.pushState] by other code, the client does not
read it: it asks the server, and shows the same page as a document load.

{1 Generated application API}

Each route is a component and owns its typed helpers:

{[
<Router.Note id search> children </Router.Note>

let destination = Router.Note.destination(~id, ())
let href = Router.Note.href(~id, ())

switch Router.useRoute() {
| Some(Router.Note({id})) => React.string(NoteId.to_string(id))
| _ => React.null
}
]}

[Router.useRoute] returns the active route as a generated variant with decoded
path parameters. It returns [None] when no route matched.

Every generated [destination], [href], and route component also takes
[?hash: string]. The router percent-encodes the value like a search value and
puts it after [#], after the search. An empty value adds no fragment:

{[
Router.Note.href(~id, ~hash="comments", ()) /* "/notes/42#comments" */
]}

A navigation keeps the hash of the destination in the URL. The request to the
server does not include it.

{2 Navigation}

[Router.useNavigation] returns the imperative navigate function and the
current navigation status:

{[
let (navigate, status) = Router.useNavigation();

let onSelect = id =>
  navigate(Router.Note.destination(~id, ())) |> ignore;

let isPending =
  switch (status) {
  | Router.Navigation.Loading(_) => true
  | Idle
  | Failed(_) => false
  };
]}

Navigating to a destination is a {e content} navigation. The client requests a
Flight model and the server answers with a full model, or with a partial patch
that reuses unchanged layouts. [~revalidate=true] forces a full response.
[~history] selects [Push], [Replace], or [Pop]. [~resetScroll=false] keeps
the scroll position and focus after a [Push] or [Replace], for a navigation
that updates the page in place, such as a refresh after a server function or a
change of filter. A hash in the destination still scrolls to its element, and
the position of the entry it leaves is still saved for Back.

A [Push] or [Replace] to a destination with the committed path and search and
a non-empty hash, without [~revalidate=true], sends no request. It commits the
hash like [updateHash] and scrolls to the fragment. A content navigation to a
URL with a hash also scrolls to and focuses the fragment's element.

A patch grafts into the outlet of the deepest scope both locations share. A
scope is shared when it declares [~layout], its path parameters are unchanged,
and the search string is unchanged. Navigating between two routes of the same
group therefore replaces only that group's outlet and leaves every enclosing
layout mounted. When a loader fails, its scope and everything below it leave
the response, and the graft moves up to the deepest scope the response still
renders.

A response without a [Router-Response] header does not come from the router,
so the client loads the target as a document. A header value other than
[full], [patch], [redirect], or [reload-required] fails the navigation. A
[Push] or [Replace] navigation then keeps the current page and sets the status
to [Failed]. A [Pop] navigation hard-navigates to the target, unless a newer
navigation has replaced it, in which case the [Pop] is canceled. The protocol
has no [failed] response kind. The protocol version is 2.

A [redirect] response continues on the client when its location has the
page's origin and is under the base path. The redirect target takes the place
of the entry the navigation would create or replace, so a [Push] that
redirects adds one entry. After five redirects in a row, or for any other
[http:] or [https:] location, the client loads the location as a document. A
location with another scheme, such as [javascript:], fails the navigation.

The default client root passes no optional provider props. To set
[~onNavigationError], [~viewTransitions] or [~interceptLinks], render
[RouterReact.Provider] from your own client component and give it to
[RouterServer.Server.clientRootWith ~make].

[RouterReact.Provider] takes [~onNavigationError], which receives every failed
navigation: its [target], a [message], and its [recovery], [StayOnPage] when
the status became [Failed] or [FullLoad] when the client loaded the target as
a document. A canceled or superseded navigation is not an error. The handler
is for development tools; the router behaves the same without it.

{3 Navigation cache}

[RouterReact.Provider ~pageCacheCapacity] keeps the navigation responses of
up to that many pages. The default is [30]. The cache keeps the response
bytes, not React elements, and decodes them again on each use. Entries are
kept apart by protocol version and registry fingerprint. Pass
[~pageCacheCapacity=0] to turn the cache and prefetching off.

The cache is on by default because, with [~pageCacheMaxAge] at [0.0], a
cached page is shown only where it cannot surprise the user: Back and Forward,
which show the page the user already saw, and a prefetched page shortly after
its request. Every other [Push] and [Replace] still asks the server.

- Back and Forward use a cached page of any age.
- [Push] and [Replace] use a cached page only when it is younger than
  [~pageCacheMaxAge] seconds. The default [0.0] means never, except for a
  prefetched page (see Prefetching).
- [~revalidate=true] does not read the cache. Its response is stored.
- Only successful pages are stored: the HTTP status and the page status are
  both 2xx. An error page, a not-found page or a 401 and 403 page is always
  asked for again.

On a miss the client sends [Router-Navigation-From] as usual, so the server
can answer with a patch. After a patch commits, the cache stores the page as
the full response of the page it was applied to, followed by the patch. The
page that the server rendered first has no stored response, so a patch on top
of it is not stored. A cached page restores its scroll position like any other
Back or Forward.

[RouterReact.useInvalidate ()] returns a function that drops entries:

{[
let invalidate = RouterReact.useInvalidate();
invalidate(All);
invalidate(Prefix(Router.Note.destination(~id, ())));
]}

[Prefix destination] drops the destination's path and every path below it,
by whole segments, whatever the search. Invalidation also stops the
prefetches in flight, and a response to a request that started before an
invalidation it falls under is not stored. After each server function call that
succeeds, the provider drops every entry by itself. Call [useInvalidate] for
other changes that cached pages show.

{3 Redirects from server functions}

A server function that ends with [RouterServer.Action.redirect destination]
answers a JavaScript call with a [Router-Redirect] header. Pass
[ReactServerDOMEsbuild.fetchCallServer ()] as the [callServer] of the
bootstrap; it posts each call (to [/] by default, or to [~url]) and reports
the header. The provider then drops the cached pages and pushes the target: a
client navigation when the target has the page's origin, is under the base
path and matches a route, otherwise a full page load. A target that is not an
[http:] or [https:] URL, such as [javascript:], is refused: the page stays
and [~onNavigationError] receives the failure.

{3 Prefetching}

When [~pageCacheCapacity] is above [0] (the default), the provider
prefetches pages into the navigation cache. A prefetch is a full navigation request with a
[Router-Prefetch] header. It never changes history or the committed page.

- [Intent]: the pointer rests on a link for 50 ms, the link gets focus, or a
  touch starts on it.
- [Viewport]: the link comes within 200 px of the viewport.
- [Idle]: the browser is idle after a navigation, for up to 15 links.

A [Link] whose route level is not [Intent] renders
[data-router-prefetch="off|viewport|idle"]. A plain anchor counts as
[Intent], and an anchor can set the attribute itself. The client asks only
when the trigger is not more eager than the link's level. At most 4
prefetches are in flight; [Intent] goes before the others. When the server
refuses, the client remembers the route's level for that URL and does not ask
again at a more eager level. Links that a click would not intercept, the
current page, a page already fresh in the cache, and connections with
[Save-Data] are not prefetched.

A [Push] or [Replace] uses a prefetched page once, within 30 seconds of its
request, even when [~pageCacheMaxAge] is [0.0]. After that use the entry is
an ordinary cached page.

{3 Scroll, focus and announcements}

The router saves the scroll position of an entry when a navigation leaves it,
including [updateSearch], [updateHash] and the page unload. Positions are kept
in [sessionStorage] for the 100 most recent entries, so Back and Forward
restore them after a reload or a full page load. When [sessionStorage] is not
available, the positions last until the page unloads. On [pagehide] the router
hands scroll restoration back to the browser, so a reload paints the saved
position before the router hydrates. Back and Forward restore
the saved position for content, search-only and hash-only entries. Without a
saved position, a hash scrolls to and focuses its element; otherwise a content
navigation focuses [#router-focus-root] when it exists and scrolls to the top,
unless it was started with [~resetScroll=false].

After each content navigation the provider writes the new [document.title]
into a visually hidden [aria-live="polite"] region, so screen readers
announce the new page.

{3 View transitions}

[RouterReact.Provider ~viewTransitions=true] commits each content navigation
inside [document.startViewTransition] when the browser supports it. During the
transition, [<html>] has [data-router-nav] set to [push], [replace], [back] or
[forward], for direction-specific CSS:

{[
html[data-router-nav="back"]::view-transition-old(root) {
  animation-name: slide-out-right;
}
]}

A navigation that starts before a transition has updated the page skips that
transition, and the page shows the newest content. The default is [false].

{3 Blocking navigation}

[RouterReact.useBlocker shouldBlock] holds navigations while [shouldBlock] is
true, for example a form with unsaved changes. It returns [Unblocked] or
[Blocked {target; action; proceed; cancel}]:

{[
switch (RouterReact.useBlocker(hasUnsavedChanges)) {
| Unblocked => React.null
| Blocked({proceed, cancel, _}) => <ConfirmLeave onLeave=proceed onStay=cancel />
};
]}

A held navigation is in the {e blocked} state until one of two calls.
[proceed ()] {e resumes} it: the navigation runs as it would have. [cancel ()]
{e cancels} it: the page stays and the navigate promise resolves to
[Canceled]. A newer navigation is held in its place and cancels the older
one. When the last blocker becomes false or unmounts, the held navigation is
canceled.

Blockers apply to [navigate] and [Link], including a [navigate] that only
changes the hash, to intercepted clicks, and to Back or Forward to other
content. Back and Forward are
undone at once with [history.go], so the address bar keeps the current URL
while the navigation is held, and [proceed] repeats the move. When the history
entry does not record its position, Back and Forward are not blocked.
[updateSearch], [updateHash], and Back or Forward between search-only and
hash-only entries do not leave the page and are not blocked. A full page load,
reload or tab close while a blocker is true shows the browser's
[beforeunload] prompt.

{2 Search updates}

[Router.useSearch] returns the decoded root search record and its update
function, which takes a whole record:

{[
let (search, updateSearch) = Router.useSearch();

let onChange = value => updateSearch({...search, text: Some(value)}) |> ignore;
]}

Search updates are {e shallow}. They update the URL and client state
synchronously and never contact the server. When server-rendered content
depends on a search value, navigate to a destination carrying the new value
instead of calling [updateSearch].

[Router.useUpdateHash] commits hash-only changes without a request.

[RouterReact.useLocation] returns the committed location: [pathname], [search]
(with its leading [?]), [hash] and [key]. On the server it reads the location
the page renders for. Like the other hooks it needs an ancestor provider.

Search values are percent-encoded the same way in destinations and in
[updateSearch]: a space is [%20], never [+], and names are sorted. One logical
query has one spelling.

{2 Links}

[RouterReact.Link] renders an [a] element that navigates on the client. Besides
[~destination], [~className], [~target], [~download] and [~ariaCurrent] it
accepts [~id], [~title], [~rel], [~ariaLabel], [~onClick] and [~onMouseEnter].
The [~onClick] handler runs first. When it calls [preventDefault], or the event
is already prevented, the router does not navigate. On the server the link
renders the same attributes and ignores both handlers. These props are on
[RouterReact.Link] only: the generated per-route [Link]s do not
take them, because they would clash with path parameters such as [id].
Both kinds of [Link] take [~history], [~revalidate] and [~resetScroll] like
[navigate]. A [Link] in a server component ignores them, because a click on it
is handled like a plain anchor.

A [Link] whose destination is the current location renders
[aria-current="page"], unless [~ariaCurrent] is given. The destination is
current when its path equals the committed path and its search has the same
names and values as the committed search, in any order and with either
spelling of a space. The hash is ignored, and a parent path is not current
for its children. Style current links with [a[aria-current="page"]].
[RouterReact.useIsCurrent destination] applies the same rule for other
elements. The comparison uses the URL: a destination does not carry its route
id.

Every [Link] without [~ariaCurrent] renders [data-router-link]. After each
committed navigation, including a patch and a search update, the provider sets
or removes [aria-current="page"] on these anchors, so links in layouts that a
patch does not render again stay correct.

A [Link] in a server component reads the location from
[RouterReact.locationScope], which the server wraps around each routed
element. The Flight payload and the HTML then carry the same
[aria-current], also after a client navigation.

{3 Plain anchors}

[RouterReact.Provider] also navigates on the client when the user clicks a
plain [<a href>] anywhere in the document, such as a link in markdown HTML or
in a server component. The click is intercepted only when all of these hold:
the left button with no modifier key, the event is not prevented, the [href]
has the page's origin, is under the base path and matches a route, the
anchor has no
[download] attribute, its [target] is absent or [_self], and the link is not a
hash on the current page. Add [data-router-reload] to an anchor to keep a full
page load. Intercepted clicks [Push] the URL.

The provider gets the route paths from the server in [~routes] and matches
the path against them with [RouterPattern.matchesPath ~basePath], without a
request. A
URL that matches no route, such as a file download under the base path,
loads as a document at once. A provider given no routes treats every URL
under the base path as a route; such a URL that the router does not serve
still loads as a document, after one request, because its response has no
[Router-Response] header. Prefetching uses the same rule.

[~interceptLinks] is [true] by default, so content outside [Link] gets the
same client navigation. Pass [~interceptLinks=false] when the application
handles anchor clicks itself.

{2 Untyped destinations}

Wrapping frameworks and server-function results cannot use generated
destination functions. [RouterRuntime.destination ~path] builds a destination
from a path that already includes its encoded search, and [RouterRuntime.href]
turns a destination back into its string. A server function returns the href
string. The client rebuilds the destination with [destination ~path] and
passes it to [navigate].

{1 Loaders}

A loader runs on the server before streaming. It receives the decoded path
parameters and ancestor loader results as labelled arguments, the scope's
search record as [~search], and returns a [Router.Loader.result]:

{[
module NoteLoader = {
  let load = (~id, ~search as _, ()) =>
    DB.fetchNoteOption(id)
    |> Lwt.map(result =>
         switch (result) {
         | Ok(Some(note)) => Router.Loader.Data(note)
         | Ok(None) => Router.Loader.NotFound
         | Error(error) => Router.Loader.Error(error)
         }
       );
};
]}

The root and its groups receive [Router.search]. A route and its page receive
[Router.Route.search]. A scope with no search parameters in reach receives no
[~search]. To read a field, annotate the argument, as in
[(~search: Router.Note.search, ...)], or qualify the field. One component
used as the page of two routes that each add search parameters receives two
different types: give each route its own component.

Loaders execute parent-first and complete before response headers or RSC
rendering begin. The scope's components receive [Data] ([~note] in the
manifest above). [NotFound] and [Error] render the nearest boundary, and
[Redirect(destination)] escapes the whole branch. [PermanentRedirect(destination)]
does the same and answers documents with status 308. Use it only for moves
that never change. Building a destination from user input, such as a [?next=]
parameter, can create an open redirect, and browsers cache 308 responses.

A loader runs again on every request, including patches, so a patch that
reuses the loader's scope keeps the layout rendered from the {e previous}
result. Use [~revalidate=true] when a navigation must show a layout rebuilt
from fresh loader data.

A patch still runs every loader on the matched branch, not only the loaders
of the scopes it sends. Child loaders receive the results of their ancestors,
so the server cannot skip an ancestor loader.

Loaders read request-local data, such as a session or a request id, from
the request the adapter holds. [DreamRouter.routes] holds the Dream request
for the whole response, including the streamed body, and
[DreamRouter.request ()] returns it in loaders, [~metadata], [~headers] and
server components. Lwt copies this storage into every callback when the
callback is registered, so each loader reads its own request after any wait,
also when requests run at the same time. Outside a request it returns [None].

{[
let load = (~id as _, ()) =>
  switch (DreamRouter.request()) {
  | Some(request) => findSession(request)
  | None => Lwt.return(Router.Loader.NotFound)
  };
]}

An adapter for another server does the same: it sets an [Lwt.key] with
[Lwt.with_value] around the {e whole} handler, including the streaming
response, not only [Server.run]. A streaming body, for example the callback
of [Dream.stream], runs after the handler returns, so a key set only around
[Server.run] is empty there.

Keep loaders for fast validation, redirects, and route-level not-found
decisions; stream slow data from async server components inside the route
elements instead.

{1 Error handling}

The root [~error] is a boundary component, like [~error] on a group or a
route. [~errorStatus] on [Router.make] maps an application error to the
response status:

{[
module AppError = {
  let status = _ => Router.Status.InternalServerError;

  [@react.component]
  let make = (~search as _, ~error as _) =>
    <p> "The requested note could not be loaded." </p>;
};

Router.make(~error=AppError, ~errorStatus=AppError.status, ...)
]}

Without [~errorStatus], an application error answers 500. The other errors
do not use it: a missing route or a loader [NotFound] answers 404, an invalid
path or search parameter 400, and an internal failure 500.

[Router.Error.t] distinguishes [NotFound], [Application], invalid path or
search parameters, and [Internal] failures carrying only a diagnostic id.
Boundary components receive the scope inputs plus [~error]. The nearest
[~notFound] handles missing routes and loader [NotFound] results. Without one,
the nearest [~error] renders instead. A path or search decode failure in a
nested scope renders the nearest ancestor [~error] boundary before any loader
at or below the failing scope runs. Root search failures use [~invalidSearch]
when declared.

When no boundary applies, the response keeps its status and the adapter
renders a built-in plain paragraph ([Not found], [Bad request], or
[Something went wrong]) through [RouterServer.Plan.resolvedElement]. It never
shows error details. [DreamRouter] uses it.

{1 Metadata and headers}

[~metadata] and [~headers] resolve per scope on every response, including
patches, and merge from the root down. Like loaders, they receive the decoded
path parameters, the search record, and ancestor loader results, and return
[Lwt] promises:

{[
module NoteMetadata = {
  let make = (~id as _, ~search as _, ~note, ()) =>
    Lwt.return(Router.Metadata.make(~title=Note.title(note), ()));
};
]}

Entries add tags to the document head. Build them with helpers:

{[
Router.Metadata.make(
  ~title="Note",
  ~entries=[
    Router.Metadata.meta(~name="robots", "noindex"),
    Router.Metadata.property(~property="og:title", "Note"),
    Router.Metadata.link(~rel="canonical", "https://example.com/note"),
    Router.Metadata.link(
      ~key="alternate:es",
      ~rel="alternate",
      ~attrs=[("hreflang", "es")],
      "https://example.com/es/note",
    ),
  ],
  (),
)
]}

An entry is [Meta { key; attr; name; content }] ([attr] is [Name] or
[Property]) or [Link { key; rel; href; attrs }]. There is no free HTML,
[<script>] or [<style>], and no [lang]: the app sets [lang] in its document
hook.

Merge works by key. A child entry removes the parent entry with the same key.
Child entries go after the remaining parent entries. The default keys are
[meta:<name>], [property:<property>] and [link:<rel>]. The prefix keeps a
[meta] and a [link] with the same word apart, and [name] apart from
[property]. A tag that repeats, such as several [alternate] links or
[og:image], needs an explicit [~key], or the last one replaces the others.
Rendering adds the React key ["router:" ^ key]. [attrs] named [rel] or [href]
are ignored: the [rel] and [href] arguments win. A [meta] named
[description] renders next to the [~description] field and does not replace
it. Use one of them.

{1 Server integration}

The native generator emits [RouterRegistry.server], a configured
[RouterServer.Server.t]. A native HTTP adapter builds a
[RouterServer.ServerEngine.request] from the request location and router
navigation headers, then calls [RouterServer.Server.run]. The adapter maps the
resulting full, patch, redirect, permanent-redirect, or reload-required outcome
to its HTTP server and streams render data through [ReactServerDOM].

[RouterServer.Adapter] holds the parts that do not depend on a server, so an
adapter only parses the request and writes the reply:

- [Adapter.reply server ~target ~pageCacheCapacity ~document ~kind outcome]
  returns [{ status; headers; body }]. [body] is [Empty], [Html element] or
  [Model { location; model }]. [headers] holds all protocol headers: [Vary:
  Accept] on every reply, [Router-Response], [Router-Registry] and
  [Cache-Control: private, no-store] for navigation replies, [Location] for
  document redirects (303, or 308 for a permanent redirect), and 409 with
  [Router-Response: reload-required] when the registry changed. The adapter
  writes [Html] with its bootstrap modules, and [Model] as a stream with
  [X-Location] set to [location] and [X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff].
- [Adapter.document_element], [Adapter.full_model], [Adapter.patch_model],
  [Adapter.redirect_model] and [Adapter.metadata_element] are the pieces
  [reply] uses. [document] is [~head -> children -> element]: the app builds
  [<html>] itself, puts [head] (the resolved metadata) in [<head>] and
  [children] in [<body>]. [document] must place [head]: the routed root does
  not render the metadata again on the server, so a document that drops
  [head] has no title or meta tags. In the browser the router renders the
  metadata itself, and React adopts the tags already in [<head>] when it
  hydrates and updates them on navigation.

Bootstrap modules and the skip-root choice are not part of the reply. The
adapter picks them for each request when it writes [Html].

The client root receives the router's settings as props. [~interceptLinks]
(default [true]) and [~viewTransitions] (default [false]) on [Router.make], or
[?interceptLinks] and [?viewTransitions] on [Server.make], reach the client
[Provider]. The client root also receives [routes]: every concrete route path
without the base path, from [Server.routes]. The client matches plain links
against them with [RouterPattern.matchesPath(~basePath)], which strips the
percent-encoded base path segment by segment. [onNavigationError] is a function, so it cannot
travel from the server in the RSC payload. To set it, render your own client
component through [Server.clientRootWith] and give the [Provider]
[~onNavigationError] there.

The repository's [demo/dream-router/DreamRouter.ml] is a thin Dream adapter
over these functions. It also parses the request and sets the request
context. [DreamRouter] is demo-only: it is not installed by any published
package.

{2 Request details}

- Base path: the server compares [~basePath] with the request pathname
  segment by segment, after percent-decoding, the same way route segments
  match. [/app] matches [/app] and [/app/x] but not [/apple], and
  [/m%c3%bcnchen] matches the base path [/m%C3%BCnchen]. The base path itself
  must be written in canonical form, without a trailing slash.
- Search: empty pairs, as in [?a=1&&b=2&], are ignored. A key or value that is
  not valid UTF-8 after percent-decoding, such as [?q=%FF], makes the search
  invalid, the same as a malformed escape: the response is 400 with
  [InvalidSearchParameter].
- Methods: [Adapter.methodReply ~allow method_] decides a method before
  [Server.run]. [allow] lists the methods the adapter serves at the router's
  paths. [OPTIONS] answers 204 and a method outside [allow] answers 405, both
  with an [Allow] header. For [None], [GET] and [HEAD] run the router, and the
  adapter sends a [HEAD] reply without its body. [DreamRouter.routes] allows
  [GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, POST] and sends [POST] to the action handler.
- [~metadata] and [~headers] of all scopes start at the same time. The results
  still merge from the root down, whatever order they resolve in.

{2 Forms without JavaScript}

A form whose [action] is a server function renders [action=""] and a hidden
[$ACTION_ID_<id>] input. Without JavaScript, the browser posts it as
multipart to the page URL. The router turns that post into
post/redirect/get, so a reload does not post again:

- The server function runs with the rest of the form. Then the server
  answers 303 to the same URL, and the browser loads the page again.
- A server function that calls [RouterServer.Action.redirect(destination)]
  gets a 303 to [destination] instead, for example
  [RouterServer.Action.redirect(Router.Note.destination(~id, ()))].
- A server function that fails renders the page, after its loaders, inside
  the nearest [~error] boundary of the route, with status 500. A route
  without a boundary shows the built-in text. Cookies set by the failed
  function are dropped. A loader's own redirect or failure still wins.
- The reply to a form post is never 307 or 308, which would make the
  browser post again. When the page redirects, for example because its URL
  ends with a slash, the reply is a 303 to the new location.

The server function's return value is not used: a page without JavaScript
cannot show it. A call from JavaScript (with the [ACTION_ID] header) returns
the Flight action response as before. When that function calls
[RouterServer.Action.redirect], the response carries
[Router-Redirect: <href>] and the result [null], and the client navigates to
[href]. The server waits for the function before it sends the headers.

[RouterServer.Adapter.runFormAction ~lookup formData] runs the function and
returns [Completed], [Redirected(destination)] or [Failed(exn)], or [None]
when the form names no registered function.
[RouterServer.Adapter.formActionReply] turns the result into the reply.
[DreamRouter.routes ~lookup] does both for a multipart [POST] without an
[ACTION_ID] header, behind the same origin check as the other actions. A
multipart [POST] without a [$ACTION_ID_] field, such as a plain upload, goes
to [~actionHandler] with its body intact. Without [~lookup], every [POST]
still goes to [~actionHandler].

{2 Redirect targets}

The server sends a redirect only to a path that starts with a single [/]
(not [//] or a slash followed by a backslash) or to an absolute [http:] or [https:] URL, without
control characters. [RouterServer.Adapter.redirectLocation] makes this check
for every redirect: the 303 or 308 [Location], the RSC redirect envelope and
the [Router-Redirect] header. A loader or route redirect to another target,
such as [javascript:alert(1)] or [//evil.example], renders the page as an
internal error. A server function that redirects there fails.

{1 Libraries}

Melange applications depend on [server-reason-react.router]. Native
applications depend on [server-reason-react.router-native]. Route generation
uses [server-reason-react.router-gen]. A native adapter that calls
[ReactServerDOM] also depends on [server-reason-react.reactDom]. There is no
installed Dream router package.

The generator reads the manifest on standard input and writes [Router.ml]
([--mode handles]), its interface [Router.mli] ([--mode interface]) and the
native [RouterRegistry.ml] ([--mode registry]). The registry refers to the
generated module as [Router]. [Router.ml] uses [RSC], so a library that
compiles it also depends on [server-reason-react.rsc] (Melange) or
[server-reason-react.rsc-native] (native):

{[
(rule
 (targets Router.ml Router.mli RouterRegistry.ml)
 (deps Routes.ml)
 (action
  (progn
   (with-stdout-to Router.ml
    (with-stdin-from %{dep:Routes.ml}
     (run %{bin:server-reason-react.router-gen} --mode handles --source %{dep:Routes.ml})))
   (with-stdout-to Router.mli
    (with-stdin-from %{dep:Routes.ml}
     (run %{bin:server-reason-react.router-gen} --mode interface --source %{dep:Routes.ml})))
   (with-stdout-to RouterRegistry.ml
    (with-stdin-from %{dep:Routes.ml}
     (run %{bin:server-reason-react.router-gen} --mode registry --source %{dep:Routes.ml}))))))
]}
